    "body": "**Before:** a flavor needed twelve hand-picked colours, and the engine only knew `dark` and `light` as flavor names.\n\n**After:**\n- `sp-theme-flavor-from-palette COLORS &key variant roles` turns a handful of `#rrggbb` colours into a flavor:\n  - the darkest colour (pushed darker) is the background of a dark theme and the lightest (pushed lighter, contrast >= 10) the foreground; the other way round for a light theme;\n  - the two most saturated remaining colours are `accent-1` (also the cursor) and `accent-2`, lifted to contrast >= 4.5;\n  - the six status hues (red ... cyan) stay recognisable but are not new colours: the usual hue blended 30% towards `accent-1`;\n  - `:roles` gives a colour to a job, e.g. `((red . 4) (blue . \"#6fa8ff\"))` (an index into the palette or a hex).\n- `sp-theme-import-palette NAME COLORS` (interactive; accepts a pasted string of colours) registers the flavor, writes `libraries/themes/sp/sp-NAME-theme.el` (three forms, the palette stays in the file) and loads it. Flavors can now carry a `:mode` (`dark`/`light`), so their names are free.\n- Example: `sp-wandavision`, from the five colours of https://www.color-hex.com/color-palette/111848 (`#2a3156 #8f8280 #4e5044 #8a4160 #8d2025`), dark: background `#171b2f`, foreground `#cdc7c6`, accents `#b77375` and `#ae7a90`.\n- The registry reads the background of such theme files.\n- Also: the registry tests expected the Modus themes and a count of 70+, which no longer matches `main` (they failed on `main`); they now match the remaining themes.\n- 4 new ERT tests; engine 13 and themes 13 pass on Emacs 29.3.\n\n**Not checked:** how `sp-wandavision` looks in a graphic frame (only that it loads, its colours, and the contrast ratios). The palette is dark and muted, so the accents are lifted a lot to stay readable; try `M-x sp-load-theme` and `sp-theme-set-color` / `:roles` to adjust. It is not in any family yet.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_013wuoefeVHQQNwZgJptsdeH)_",
